===Etymology 1===
Origin uncertain; perhaps from {{suffix|en|gaud|t1=ornament, trinket|y}}, perhaps ultimately from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|gaudir||to rejoice}}.

Alternatively, from {{inh|en|enm|gaudi}}, {{m|enm|gawdy|t=yellowish}}, from {{der|en|fro|gaude}}, {{m|fro|galde|t=weld (the plant)}}, from {{der|en|frk|*walda}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*walþō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*walþijō}}, akin to {{cog|ang|*weald}}, {{m|ang|*wielde}} (>{{cog|enm|welde}}, {{m|enm|wolde}} and Anglo-{{cog|la|walda|t=alum}}), {{cog|gml|wolde}}, {{cog|dum|woude}}. More at {{cog|en|weld}}.

A common claim that the word derives from [[w:Antoni Gaudí|Antoni Gaudí]], designer of Barcelona's [[w:Sagrada Família|Sagrada Família Basilica]], is incorrect: the word was in use centuries before Gaudí was born.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# very [[showy]] or [[ornamented]], now especially when [[excessive]], or in a [[tasteless]] or [[vulgar]] manner
#* Shakespeare
#*: Costly thy habit as thy purse can buy, / But not expressed in fancy; rich, not '''gaudy'''.
#* '''1813''', [[w:Jane Austen|Jane Austen]], ''Pride and Prejudice''
#*: The rooms were lofty and handsome, and their furniture suitable to the fortune of its proprietor; but Elizabeth saw, with admiration of his taste, that it was neither '''gaudy''' nor uselessly fine; with less of splendour, and more real elegance, than the furniture of Rosings.
#* '''1887''', Homer Greene, ''Burnham Breaker''
#*: A large '''gaudy''', flowing cravat, and an ill-used silk hat, set well back on the wearer's head, completed this somewhat noticeable costume.
#* '''2005''', Thomas Hauser & Marilyn Cole Lownes, "How Bling-bling Took Over the Ring", ''The Observer'', 9 January 2005
#*: '''Gaudy''' jewellery might offend some people's sense of style. But former heavyweight champion and grilling-machine entrepreneur George Foreman is philosophical about today's craze for bling-bling.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[gay]]; [[merry]]; [[festive]]
#: {{rfquotek|Tennyson}}
#* Shakespeare
#*: Let's have one other '''gaudy''' night.
#* Twain
#*: And then, there he was, slim and handsome, and dressed the gaudiest and prettiest you ever saw...

